## 8. Landmarks/headings — PASS (point 1)

`h1Count=1, main=1, nav=1, header=1` on `/whatif`.

## 9. Contrast, both themes — PASS (points 7/12)

Fixed a bug in my own probe first: the outer `[role="alert"]` div doesn't
itself carry the `text-negative` color class (only its nested `<p>`
does), so my first pass measured inherited black-on-purple in dark mode.
Corrected to read the inner text node's computed color:
- Light: text `rgb(190,18,60)` on bg `rgb(255,241,242)` → **5.72:1**.
- Dark: text `rgb(253,164,175)` on bg `rgb(41,23,42)` → **8.88:1**.
- Border vs bg ≥3:1 both themes (5.72:1 light, 8.88:1 dark — border and
  text share the same `text-negative`/`border-negative` token here).
Matches attempt 2's numbers exactly (unchanged markup/CSS).

## 10. Reflow at 320/375 — PASS for new/changed content; one PRE-EXISTING,
    out-of-scope defect reconfirmed with identical provenance

- 375px: doc `scrollWidth`=375=`innerWidth`; error fragment `scrollWidth
  === clientWidth` (297), no internal clipping.
- 320px: doc `scrollWidth`=344 (24px overflow). Reproduced on a **fresh
  page load with no error ever triggered** (baseline probe): identical
  344px, and `document.querySelectorAll('body *')` walk confirms the
  widest element is the Big-Ticket "Add Item"
  `<button class="px-3 py-1 bg-accent-strong...">` — same element,
  same 344px, as WS4.1's and WS4.2's identical findings. Untouched by
  WS4.2.diff AND WS4.3.diff (that file — `bigticket-card.html` — is
  byte-identical since master). OBSERVATION, not a FAIL: pre-existing,
  out of this task's scope.

